# The annotations of Enduro Racer's image at the entry $8000 ($5B00-$FFFF, work/stage1/images/entry.cpu.bin, c1
# frame 11646 T 14544): the source of src/game/*.asm (../../kit/mkgame.py). Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
# The recordings (WALKTHROUGH 6.0): c1 (a 48K from power-on, the TOSEC [SpeedLock 2] copy at signal speed, the menu,
# stage 1), m128 (the same on a 128K), stages (all seven POKEs: the five stages, the end, the tables, the name entry),
# ctl (every control method, DEFINE KEYS), crash (falls at speed), twoplayer (two players, turns), reset3 (the stage 3
# reset). "Known only from the code" marks what no recording shows.

@module entry ; Mover, the entry, the IM 2 table and the 128K test
@about The last tape block puts Mover at $5B00; LoadEnd ($FF7A, src/loader/tail.asm) jumps there. Mover's LDDR moves the 40 540 bytes $5BC4-$FA1F up by $5E0 to $61A4-$FFFF (the tape blocks were cut with that gap) and jumps to Start at $8000. Init sets I = $B2 and fills the IM 2 table $B200-$B300 with $64 (every vector reads $6464: JR Interrupt), copies the 13 record bytes to $5E00 and the 1456 bytes of track tables from $EB00 to $5E80 (the back buffer takes $EB00 after that), builds the bit-reverse table at $5D00, tests the ROM, and starts the attract loop.
@about The work space below $6430 ($5B00-$642F) is overwritten before any read: the image's bytes there are leftovers (Mover, zeros, the LDDR's leftover, the image's old table bytes), and each entry is named after what the game keeps there.
@about The 128K test is a checksum: the byte sum of the ROM's $3B00-$3BFF is $FE on the 48K ROM; then Beeper ($7C2F) = $FF and the engine sounds on the beeper, else 0 and the engine is an AY tone (m128: $7C2F = 0, the AY written by EngineSound).
$5B00 c Mover ; LDDR $5BC4-$FA1F up by $5E0 to $61A4-$FFFF, SP = $61A7, JP Start
> Loaded by tape block 15 (row 9 of BlockTable, src/loader/loader.asm) and entered by TailEnd's JP $5B00 at c1 frame 11632 (m128 too). DE = $FFFF, HL = $FA1F, BC = $9E5C (40 540), LDDR; SP = $61A7, the top of the gap the move leaves; JP Start. The entry image differs from the loaded one only by that move (doc 02, 2.2).
> Witness: L1 1379 (c1, m128).
@isub $5B03 LD HL,$FA1F
@isub $5B0B LD SP,$61A7
$5B11 b MoverTail ; The 47 bytes after Mover in tape block 15: the ROM font's letters F to K, never read
> Tape block 15 carries 64 bytes; Mover uses 17. $5B11-$5B3F are the Spectrum ROM's glyphs of F, G, H, I, J and K (ROM $3E31-$3E5F; the first row of F, a zero, falls on JP Start's last byte). The game writes RowList ($5B00) before it reads it; nothing reads these bytes as loaded (c1, m128). A leftover of the tape's cutting.
@alias RowList Mover ; the road's row list $5B00-$5B7F, written over Mover in play
$5B40 s RowListTail ; $5B40-$5B7F: zeros no tape block loads; the rest of RowList (RoadRowsMake writes it)
> RowList ($5B00-$5B7F, over Mover) is the list of the road's rows, each the RoadLines index of a drawn line, $FE a gap (GapRows), $FF the end; RoadRowsMake ($9DF1) writes it, DrawRoad reads it.
$5B80 b BendRows ; The bend row table, 128 bytes: each road row's x centre (the bend's sideways shift), built by ScrollBends / BlendBend
> In the image $5B80-$5BC7 are zeros (no block loads them) and $5BC8-$5BFF the start of the LDDR's leftover: an LDDR moving a block up leaves its first $5E0 bytes where they were, so $5BC8-$61A3 still hold what is now at $61A8-$6783 (entry.cpu.bin byte for byte; tools/parts.py rebuilds it so). ClearBendRows and the game overwrite all of $5B80-$642F before reading it.
$5C00 b RoadLines ; The screen line of each road row, 128 bytes ($70 + depth / 2): RoadRowsFlat, RoadRowsCrest, RoadRowsJump write it
> RoadLines' last entry ($5C7F) gives RoadTopRow = ($5C7F) - $71; all entries $70 (the road flat under a high jump) make RoadTopRow $FF (the stage 3 reset, leftovers section of NOTES).
$5C80 b Riders 15 ; The six riders, 15 bytes each: flags, x, distance, speed, pose, jump
> +0 flags (bit 7 not in the race, bit 0 the second kind: the wider sprite set and the hard collision), +2 x, +3/+4 distance (the high byte is the road row; 0-$4F on screen), +5/+6 speed, +7 acceleration, +8 top speed, +9 pose (0-3, 4 for the second kind), +$0A the lowest pose on a hill, +$0B/+$0C jump height, +$0D/+$0E jump speed. StartRace fills it from RiderStarts; MoveRiders and DrawRiders use it.
$5CDA DrawStack ; the track interpreter's stack for the drawing walk (a copy of TrackStack each pass, 13 bytes)
$5CE7 SavedSp2 ; DrawRoad's SP while it walks RowList (and RoadRowsJump's)
$5CE9 JumpHeight ; the bike's height in the air, 8.8 ($5CEA the whole part; 0 on the ground)
$5CEF HorizonAddr ; the stage's horizon (StageTable)
$5CF1 HorizonRows ; its rows
$5CF2 HorizonBottom ; the picture's top line (StageTable byte 3): DrawHorizonAt clears from TopClip down to it, then copies the picture from it (the bottom of the clear, not of the picture)
$5CF3 HorizonScroll ; the horizon's scroll position, 8.8 (HorizonScrollStep)
$5CF5 JumpRows ; up to four rows of ramps (type 0 objects) this pass, $FF the end: MoveRiders makes riders jump there
$5CF9 JumpRowPtr ; the next free JumpRows place
$5CFB DrawTrackPtr ; the drawing walk's track position
$5CFD JumpSpeed ; the bike's vertical speed, 8.8 ($5CFE the whole part), minus $02F0 each pass
$5CFF SplashOn ; $87 on stages 2 and 4: the splash under the bike (DrawSplash)
$5D00 b ReverseTable 16 ; Each byte with its bits reversed: Init builds it, DrawMirrored reads it (H = $5D)
$5E00 b Records ; The five stage records (seconds, hundredths, BCD) and their total (minutes, seconds, hundredths)
> Init copies InitialRecords here; RecordStages improves them at game over. They last until the machine is reset.
$5E0D b Player ; The current player's block, 56 bytes (NewGame clears it; TurnLoop swaps it with OtherPlayer)
$5E0D PlayerChar ; '1' or '2' (the HUD's nUP)
$5E0E StageTimes ; five stage times: seconds, hundredths (BCD)
$5E18 Score ; seven characters, leading blanks
$5E1F Timer ; seconds left, BCD (game over at 0)
$5E20 Tenths ; passes to the next second, 9 down to 0 (TimerTick reads it first: at 0 the timer's step and 10 again, then one off)
$5E21 TimerAtStart ; Timer and Tenths at the stage's start (StageTime)
$5E23 Stage ; 0-4 (5 after the finish)
$5E24 Countdown ; the start's countdown (16 down; bit 7: resume after a fall)
$5E25 SegmentLeft ; the segment's progress, 8.8: counts down by ScrollStep
$5E27 TrackPtr ; the track position (Track's elements)
$5E29 TrackDistance ; the distance into the track, 8.8
$5E2B StageListPtr ; the segment in StageList
$5E2D BendDir ; the bend's side (segment bit 4): 1 one way, 0 the other
$5E2E BendPrev ; the previous segment's bend (bits 0-1: 0 straight, 1-3 a bend profile)
$5E2F Bend ; the segment's bend (bits 0-1)
$5E30 CrestPrev ; the previous segment's crest (bits 2-3: 0 level, 1-3 a rise or dip)
$5E31 Crest ; the segment's crest (bits 2-3)
$5E32 FallX ; x at a fall (the restart's x)
$5E33 GapMarks ; the rows of the two $F0 marks (the gap ends); $7F none
$5E35 Segments ; segments driven in the stage (the lap table's %)
$5E36 VarObject ; the object type the $B0 command set (elements of type 10 draw it)
$5E37 TrackStack ; the track interpreter's stack: depth, two frames of 6 bytes (return, length, distance)
$5E45 b OtherPlayer ; The other player's block, 56 bytes, swapped in by TurnLoop
$5E7D b PlayerPad ; 3 bytes after the blocks, never used ($66, $64, $00 in the image: LDDR leftovers)
$5E80 b StageList ; The stage list, one byte a segment (Init copies it from $EB00; the image's own bytes here are leftovers)
> bits 0-1 the bend (0 straight, BendProfiles 1-3), bits 2-3 the crest (0 level, CrestTable 1-3), bit 4 the direction, bit 5 the stage's last segment, bits 6-7 added to the segment's progress at its start (SegmentLen, $AF81). Five stages end at $5E9F, $5EB6, $5ED0, $5EEB, $5F0B: 32, 23, 26, 27 and 32 segments. src/res/track/stage1.txt ... stage5.txt (tools/course.py).
$5F0C TrackPatterns ; the patterns the $E0 commands run
$60D0 Track ; the track's elements, from the start of stage 1 (TrackPtr)
$6430 b AySilence ; AY register 7 = $3F: every tone and noise off (written when the tune stops)
> AY data: a register then its value, written by AyWrite ($6806). Interrupt writes this one pair when the tune request TuneRequest goes to 0 (pause, the end of a race).
$6432 AyTuneStart ; four pairs at a tune's start: 7 = $38 (tones A, B, C on), 8 = $0D, 9 = $0E, 10 = $0F (volumes)
$6438 AyEngineVolume ; register 10 (volume C) = $0F: EngineSound's AY tone on channel C
$643A TuneHeld ; nonzero while an effect owns the AY: the tune's mixer is written again when it ends
$643B TunePlaying ; the tune the channels play: 0 none, 1 or 2 (compared with TuneRequest each interrupt)

@module interrupt ; The IM 2 handler: its own stack, the repair of the two bytes its push wrote, the sound
@about Every vector of the table at $B200 reads $6464, JR Interrupt. The main code draws with SP as a data pointer (DrawRoad pops row addresses from RowTable $FE00, DrawHorizon pushes zeros into the back buffer, BlitRows pops rows), so the return address the interrupt pushes lands in that data. Interrupt saves SP in IntSavedSp, takes its own stack (IntStack, $6574-$6583), reads the return address from where it was pushed into the operand of the JP at IntReturn, and repairs the two bytes: inside RowTable ($FE00-$FF7F, and $FF80-$FF81, which the L test lets through) it writes back the row address that belongs there (RowEntry), anywhere else (the back buffer, where the pushes write zeros) it writes zeros; at the real stack ($FF82-$FFFF) it leaves them. It returns by EI and the patched JP, not RET.
@about Then the sound: a change of TuneRequest at a tune's mark starts the other tune (the two AY channels, PlayChannel) or silences it; the channels step every interrupt; EngineSound runs every second one. One entry a frame (c1: 1.00 a frame over play).
@about The repair has a hole: when SP points into the ROM ($0000-$3FFF) the push is lost and the JP returns to the word the ROM holds there. DrawRoad does that when the road's top row leaves the table (the stage 3 reset, reset3 frame 6759: RoadTopRow = $FF, SP = $00DE + 2n, IntSavedSp = $010A, the JP to $3CBD).
$643C c Interrupt ; Save SP, take IntStack, read the return address into IntReturn's JP, push the registers
> Entered through IntVector ($6464, JR here) on every interrupt in play and the menus. IntSavedSp = SP; SP = $6584; AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY pushed on IntStack. HL = the interrupted SP: the return address is read from (HL) into the operand of IntReturn's JP ($6524), IntSavedSp += 2 (the SP to restore), HL = the address of the two bytes the push wrote; A = H, and on at IntRepair.
> Recorded: c1, m128 and every play recording; 1.00 entries a frame in c1's play.
> Witness: L1 79550 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$645F b SkidLevel ; The AY skid noise: 0 none, else EngineSound's noise period (set by OffRoad, the dust on stage 3)
$6460 TuneMark ; 1 after a tune's $CA mark: the tune may change now (PlayChannel sets it, Interrupt clears it)
$6461 TuneRequest ; the tune the game wants: 0 silence, 1 (odd stages) or 2 (even), set by SetTune; also gates EngineSound
$6462 EffectTime ; the AY effect's count of interrupts (0: none); AyEffectTick counts it down
$6463 EffectStep ; the effect's step, the AY register 10 sweep
$6464 c IntVector ; JR Interrupt (the vector of every IM 2 entry); then the repair and the sound part of the handler
> $6464 is the address every byte of the IM 2 table $B200-$B300 points at ($64,$64). The bytes after the JR are the handler's second half, reached by Interrupt's JP $6466 (IntRepair).
> IntRepair ($6466): H = $FE, or H = $FF and L < $82: RowEntry recomputes the address that belongs at that place of RowTable; H elsewhere but $FF: the two bytes become 0; the real stack ($FF82 up): nothing. IntSound ($6476): TuneRequest against TunePlaying; a change waits for TuneMark, then TunePlaying = TuneRequest, and 0 writes AySilence, 1 or 2 starts the tune (TuneTable's two streams into ChannelA and ChannelB, as stream and restart, counts 1, repeat depths $FF, TuneMark 0, the four pairs of AyTuneStart, AyMixer's value $38). A tune playing: on the 128K (Beeper 0) an effect (EffectTime) holds the channels (TuneHeld = EffectTime); its end writes AyTuneStart and AyMixer again; else both channels step (PlayChannel, ChannelA then ChannelB). TuneRequest nonzero: EngineSound. IntExit pops, SP = IntSavedSp, EI, JP to the interrupted code.
@isub $6499 LD DE,TuneTable-4
@isub $655A LD HL,$A600
$6466 IntRepair ; A = H of the pushed bytes' address: $FE or $FF below $82 is RowTable, elsewhere zeros
$6476 IntSound ; the tune change, the channels, the engine
$6516 IntExit ; pop the registers, SP = IntSavedSp, EI
$6523 IntReturn ; JP nn: nn is the interrupted return address, written by Interrupt
$6526 IntZeroBytes ; outside RowTable: the two bytes the push wrote become 0
$652E RowEntry ; inside RowTable: entry n = (address - $FE00) / 2 gets its row address again
> n < $28: the screen line n's address (LineAddress); n < $40: $EB00 + 32 (n - $28), a back buffer row; n >= $40: the screen line n's address + $A600, a back buffer row. What SetRowTable left there in play.
> Witness: L1 79550 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$6566 b IntStack ; The handler's stack, $6566-$6585 (SP = $6584 at the entry): six pushes and its calls; zeros in the image
> WALKTHROUGH 1 (the stack-flagged bytes of c1's L1) gives $6574-$6583 as the bytes the handler writes.
$6586 IntSavedSp ; the interrupted SP + 2 (the SP the handler restores); the image holds $B0ED, an old value

@module sound ; Sound: the AY tunes, the effects, the engine on the beeper or the AY
@about The AY plays a two-channel tune in play, tune 1 on odd stages and tune 2 on even ones (SetTune), switched only at a $CA mark of the tune (TuneMark). The code writes the AY ports on the 48K too, where nothing answers (c1: the writes; m128: the AY's registers move). PlayChannel reads a channel's byte stream: a note is a length byte below $C8 then the note byte (low nibble 1-12 a semitone of NoteTable, 0 a rest; high nibble plus the channel's transpose: how many times the period is halved); commands: $C9 restart, $CA the mark, $FE n repeat n times, $FD the repeat's end, $FC addr a call, $FB return, any other byte a count of AY pairs inline (no tune uses that).
@about The engine (EngineSound, every second interrupt while a tune is requested): on the 48K a burst of 11 beeper edges whose spacing falls as the speed rises (plus a ROM byte AND $3F as jitter); on the 128K a tone on AY channel C from the speed, or the skid noise (SkidLevel). The speed is folded before use (below 60 as is, 60-129 minus 60, from 130 minus 120): the note drops twice as the bike speeds up, a gear change heard. The effects and beeps are in the game's modules: PlayEffect ($A179) and AyEffectTick ($ACA5), the AY noise of a fall or a bounce; CrashSound ($AC88), the 48K's beeper noise, BeepTone ($AB95), the name entry's sweep NameEntrySound ($8AF0).
$6588 c PlayChannel ; Step one tune channel (IX): count its note down, then read the next note or command
> In: IX = ChannelA or ChannelB. The count (IX+0) minus 1: reaching 0 sets the channel's mixer bit (IX+$0E) in AyMixer's value (the note stops for one interrupt) and writes the mixer; going past 0 ($FF) toggles the bit back and reads the stream at (IX+1,2): a length byte below $C8 (the new count, minus 1), then the note byte: low nibble 0 sets the bit (a rest); 1-11 reads NoteTable's word n ($66AA + 2n), halved (high nibble + transpose IX+3) times, into the channel's tone registers (IX+$0D and +1). Bytes from $C8: ChannelCommands.
> Recorded: c1, m128: two calls each interrupt in play.
> Witness: L1 65445 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$65B1 ChannelRead ; the next byte of the stream
$65DA ; NoteTable ($66AA) + 2n never crosses $66FF, so the INC H below is never needed
$65DC c NoteCarry ; INC H for a note table that crosses a page: not reached with this table
> Known only from the code.
$65DD c ChannelPeriod ; The period from (HL), halved (high nibble + transpose) times, out to the channel's tone registers
> In: HL = the word in NoteTable, C = the note byte, IX the channel. Writes the low then the high register (IX+$0D, IX+$0D+1) through ports $FFFD / $BFFD.
> Witness: L1 6397 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$660E ChannelCommands ; $C9 restart at (IX+4,5) and clear TuneMark; $CA set TuneMark; $FE n repeat; $FD repeat end; $FB return; $FC call
$668B c ChannelAyPairs ; Any other command byte: the next byte E is a count of AY pairs, written by AyWrite, then on
> Known only from the code: no stream of the two tunes holds such a byte (src/res/sound/tunes.txt lists every command they use).
$6694 c ChannelLoop ; IY = the channel's repeat record: IX + 7 + 3 * depth (count, address)
> Witness: L1 2936 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$66AA w NoteTable 12 ; The AY tone periods of one octave ($0EEE ... $07E9), halved per octave by ChannelPeriod
> A note n reads word n ($66AA + 2n): word 0 ($0EEE) is never read, and a note 12 would read TuneTable; the tunes use the notes 2, 4, 6, 7, 9 and 11 (src/res/sound/tunes.txt).
@res sound/notes.bin
$66C2 w TuneTable 4 ; The tunes' streams, two words a tune: tune 1 Tune1A, Tune1B; tune 2 Tune2A, Tune2B
> Read at IntSound as $66BE + 4 * tune (the operand TuneTable-4): tune 0 is silence and has no row.
$66CA b Tune2A ; Tune 2, channel A: calls of phrases, repeats and $CA marks (the stream format: PlayChannel)
> src/res/sound/tunes.txt lists the four streams as text (sound/tunes.bin, tools/res_formats.py).
@res sound/tunes.bin
$6701 Tune1A ; tune 1, channel A
$6725 Tune2B ; tune 2, channel B
$6790 Tune1B ; tune 1, channel B
$67FB b AyMixer ; AY register 7 and its value: the channels' mixer (PlayChannel sets and clears the tone bits)
> Pairs for AyWrite: register, value. AyMixer's value ($67FC) is the live mixer: PlayChannel, EngineTone and EngineSkid change its bits and WriteMixer sends it.
$67FD AyVolumeC ; register 10 (channel C's volume), set by EngineSkid from SkidLevel
$67FF AyNoise ; register 6 (the noise period), set by EngineSkid from the speed
$6801 c WriteMixer ; AY register 7 = AyMixer's value
> Witness: L1 16090 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$6806 c AyWrite ; Write E pairs (register, value) from HL to the AY: OUT ($FFFD) the register, OUT ($BFFD) the value
> Out: HL past the pairs. On the 48K the ports reach nothing (the game writes them on both machines).
> Witness: L1 18027 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$6817 b ChannelA ; Tune channel A: count, stream, transpose, restart, repeat depth and records, tone register 2 (AY channel B), mixer bit 1 (value $02)
> 17 bytes: +0 the note's count, +1/+2 the stream pointer, +3 the transpose, +4/+5 the restart, +6 the repeat depth ($FF none), +7 two records of 3 bytes (count, address), +$0D the tone register, +$0E the mixer bit, +$0F/+$10 the call's return.
$6828 ChannelB ; tune channel B: tone register 0 (AY channel A), mixer bit 0 (value $01)
$6839 c LineAddress ; DE = the screen address of pixel line A (L = the same line): the ROM's PIXEL-ADD layout, column 0
> In: A = L = the line (0-191). D = $40 + (line AND 7) + 8 * (line >> 6), E = (line AND $38) * 4. RowEntry's helper.
> Witness: L1 13451 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$6851 c EngineSound ; Every second interrupt: the engine's note from SPEED, on the beeper (48K) or AY channel C (128K)
> EngineToggle (the LD A operand at $6852) flips each call and the call with it 0 returns. A = SPEED ($7C34), +9 in the air (JumpHeight), folded (below 60 as is, 60-129 minus 60, then minus 60 more), C = $63 - A. 48K (Beeper nonzero): 11 edges on port $FE, the n-th after $14 + C + (a ROM byte at $02xx AND $3F) DJNZ turns: a buzz whose pitch rises with the speed. 128K: an effect playing (EffectTime): nothing; SkidLevel nonzero: EngineSkid; else EngineTone.
> Recorded: c1: the OUT ($FE) at $687F in play; m128: the AY path $68D1.
> Witness: L1 28577 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$6852 EngineToggle ; flips 0 / $FF each call
$687F EngineEdges ; 48K: the burst
$689E c EngineSkid ; 128K: channel C's volume from SkidLevel, the noise period from SPEED, noise on C and tone off
> In: A = SkidLevel. Volume = ((A >> 2) AND 3) + $0B, 11-14 (the clamp to 15 never fires); noise period = SPEED >> 4 (SPEED 0: EngineTone instead); AyVolumeC and AyNoise written, mixer AND $1F OR 4.
> Known only from the code: SkidLevel is set by the sand of stage 3 (OffRoadSand), and no recording left the road there on the 128K.
$68D1 c EngineTone ; 128K: AY channel C's period from C and SPEED's low byte, volume 15 (AyEngineVolume), tone C on
> Period = (C + $1C) * 32 with the top bits of -SPEED's low byte ($7C33) shifted in. Mixer AND $3B OR $20.
> Witness: L1 1768 (m128).

@module graphics ; The graphics and tables: sprites, the font, the perspective and edge tables, the big digits, the bend profiles, the horizons, the track tables
@about Everything here is read through the descriptors and routines named in each entry; the editable forms are in src/res (each folder's README). Bytes no routine reads are kept as documented leftovers.
$690F b Leftover690F ; $690F-$6BE7: 729 bytes no instruction reads in any recording (a repeated word pattern, an older table)
> L1's byte use has no read here in the seven recordings; no instruction's operand points into it.
$6BE8 b ObjectSprites ; The roadside objects' sprites (ObjectTypes' lists point into $6BE8-$75AF), and the bike's dust and splash elsewhere
> src/res/sprites/objects.png and .csv (tools/res_formats.py: every descriptor's sprite on a sheet).
@res sprites/objects.bin
$75B0 b RoadRowsOldData ; 39 bytes RoadRowsOld points IX at: read by no recording
$75D7 b BendBlend ; BlendBend's steps: 16 rows of slowly rising values (49 bytes to $7607)
@res tables/bendblend.bin
$7608 b Font 8 ; The 8x8 font, characters $20-$5E (PrintChar's base $7508 = Font - $100: code - $20 rows of 8)
> 63 characters; a $5F would read Perspective's first bytes. The frame pieces, the arrows, km/h and UP are among them (src/res/font/font.png, the order of the codes).
@res font/font.bin
$7800 b Perspective 16 ; Each of the 128 road rows' depth (the far row first: $9F ... $11), the road's perspective
@res tables/perspective.bin
$7880 b EdgePatterns 24 ; The road edge's 3-byte patterns, 8 pixel shifts for each of 16 widths (DrawRoad, near rows)
@res tables/edges.bin
$7A00 b CrestTable 16 ; The crest's change of each row's screen line (RoadRowsCrest: perspective * crest table / 512)
@res tables/crest.bin
$7A80 b EdgePatterns2 24 ; The second set of edge patterns (DrawRoad, chosen by bit 2 of the row)
@res tables/edges2.bin
$7C00 b CountGlyphs 8 ; The countdown's glyphs: G, O (8 lines each), then 1, 2, 3 (10 lines each) for CountGlyph
@res tables/countglyphs.bin
$7C2E b PassCount ; The passes, + 1 each (the wheel animation's and the HUD's beat)
> The variables $7C2E-$7C53 are zeros in the image.
$7C2F Beeper ; $FF on the 48K (the ROM test), 0 on the 128K
$7C30 DemoMode ; a demo's segments left (8-15 at the start), 0 in a game
$7C31 GetUp ; the getting-up slide (LateralX toward 0)
$7C32 Players ; 1 or 2
$7C33 Speed ; SPEED, 8.8 km/h ($7C34 the whole part, 0-199: the throttle's curve nears $C800 and never reaches it)
$7C35 TotalTime ; the player's total (minutes, seconds, hundredths)
$7C38 SavedSp ; SP while a routine uses it as a pointer
$7C3A InGap ; the bike over stage 3's gap (GapRows)
$7C3B ; unused (2 bytes)
$7C3C ScoreDigits ; 7 digits (0-9) AddScore adds, read back from $7C42: the speed's each pass, the time bonus at $7C3D (with $7C3F-$7C42 as the last ScoreAdd left them: the bonus carries that pass's speed points)
$7C43 ; unused
$7C46 RunLines ; DrawRoadRun's work: lines, x positions and slopes ($7C46-$7C52)
$7C53 ExtendedTime ; EXTENDED PLAY's flashing passes
$7C54 b BigDigits 2 ; The ten big digits, 15 lines of 2 bytes (16 x 15 pixels) each (BigDigit)
@res font/bigdigits.bin
$7D80 b BendProfiles 16 ; The three bends' profiles, each row's sideways shift, 128 rows each (ScrollBends: $7D80 + 128 * (bend - 1))
