@module render ; The back buffer, RowTable, the sprite blitters, the copy to the screen
@about RowTable ($FE00-$FF7F) holds the address of each of the 192 pixel lines. SetRowTable (DE) adds DE to lines 64-191 and rebuilds lines 40-63, so the same table points at the screen (DE = $5A00, back from the buffer) or at the back buffer (DE = $A600): lines 40-63 are $EB00 + 32 (n - 40), lines 64-191 the screen address + $A600 ($EE00-$FDFF in the screen's own order). Lines 0-39 (the HUD) always point at the screen. Every drawing routine goes through RowTable: the printer, the road, the sprites; StartRace switches it to the back buffer and RaceEnd back.
@about The blitters (BlitRows, the core of DrawSprite and DrawShifted) take SP = RowTable + 2 * line and POP each line's address, OR a sprite row into it, clipped at line 40 (TopClip) and at the screen's sides. An interrupt pushes its return address into RowTable then; Interrupt puts the entry back (RowEntry). DrawShifted first copies the sprite's rows into the line buffer at $8000 shifted by x AND 7 (an unrolled RR / RL chain entered at the width's place); DrawMirrored ($970C) copies them mirrored through ReverseTable.
@about CopyBuffer copies the back buffer to the screen each pass: from the highest line anything was drawn on (TopClip, DrawnTop) to line 63 line by line, then lines 64-191 in one block ($EE00 to $4800, 4096 bytes). BlitCopyPop, the POP copy the stage table's byte 7 could select, never runs (byte 7 is 0 on all five stages).
$8E7D c SetRowTable ; RowTable's lines 64-191 += DE; lines 40-63: the screen's (DE = $5A00) or the back buffer's rows
> In: DE = $A600 (to the back buffer) or $5A00 (back to the screen: $A600 + $5A00 = $10000). Lines 40-63 get lines 168-191's addresses minus $1000 (the screen's rows 40-63), and with DE not $5A00 then $EB00 + 32 n.
> Recorded: c1: StartRace $A466 and RaceEnd $AAA8 each stage.
> Witness: L1 48 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, truck, twoplayer).
@isub $8EA1 LD DE,-$1000
$8ED8 c ClearBendRows ; BendRows = 0 ($5B80-$5C00, 129 bytes through CopyRows)
> Witness: L1 175 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$8EE4 c CopyRows ; Copy A x 32 bytes HL -> DE (32 unrolled LDI a turn)
> Witness: L1 1004349 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$8EE6 CopyRows31 ; the first turn one LDI shorter (ClearBendRows' fill)
$8F29 c DrawSprite ; A byte-aligned sprite: C bytes wide, B rows from DE, at x H (carry: x < 0), bottom line L
> Sets the core's skip (BlitSkip, $9104) and first column ($906A, $90FE) from x, clipping at the left edge (RET when wholly off), then BlitRows.
> Witness: L1 13978 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$8F53 c DrawShifted ; A sprite at any x: its rows shifted by x AND 7 into the line buffer at $8000, then BlitRows
> In as DrawSprite. Row by row: the bytes copied (the unrolled LDI chain entered by the patched DJNZ at $8FE7), then RR (HL) or RL (HL) chains shift the row (x AND 7 below 4: right; else left by 8 - n). Byte-aligned x goes straight to BlitRows.
> Recorded: c1: the bike, the riders and the objects every pass.
> Witness: L1 50169 (c1, crash, ctl, fall5, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
@isub $8F93 LD HL,LineBuffer+1
@isub $8FA3 LD DE,LineBuffer
$8FCB c ShiftCopyHead ; The first three LDI of the row copy: entered only for rows of 12 bytes or more
> Known only from the code: no sprite drawn shifted is that wide.
$8FD1 ShiftCopy ; the row copy (entered at its width)
$8FF8 c ShiftRightHead ; The first two RR (HL) of the right shift: rows of 12 bytes or more
> Known only from the code.
@isub $9023 LD HL,LineBuffer+1
$8FFE ShiftRight ; the right shift chain
$9033 c ShiftLeftHead ; The first two RL (HL) of the left shift: rows of 12 bytes or more
> Known only from the code.
$9039 ShiftLeft ; the left shift chain
$9069 BlitRows ; the core: SP = RowTable + 2 * line, POP each line, OR the row, clipped at 32 columns
$90B3 c BlitHead ; The first OR of the unrolled row: rows of 13 bytes
> Known only from the code.
$90B8 BlitRow ; the unrolled OR of one row (entered at its width)
$90FD BlitNext ; next line: SP pops it
$9104 BlitSkip ; LD BC,nn: nn = the bytes skipped a row (the clipped part)
$910C ; SP back (SavedSp)

@module objects ; The roadside objects, the riders, the splash and the dust
@about Each pass the track is walked a second time from the bike's place for the drawing (RaceLoop's object walk, $A70F): every element ahead within the visible rows is drawn by DrawObject with its type's descriptor list (ObjectTypes), the list entry picked by the row (the farther, the smaller sprite). Type 0 is a ramp: its rows go to JumpRows for the riders. The near elements are tested against the bike (CollisionSizes).
@about Six riders (Riders) run their own race: MoveRiders gives each a speed toward its top speed, moves it against the bike's ScrollStep, leans its pose in bends and makes it jump at the ramps; DrawRiders draws those on the visible rows with RiderSprites, mirrored by the bend's side unless the sprite is symmetric. Riders 4 and 5 are the trucks (flags bit 0, pose 4): rider 4 in the race from stage 2, rider 5 from stage 4; both in a demo; flags bit 7 out (StartRace).
$9111 b RiderStarts 4 ; The six riders' starts: x, acceleration, top speed, the lowest pose in a bend (the sixth's last two bytes are BikePoses' first)
> StartRace copies 4 bytes a rider; the sixth reads $9127-$9128 (6, $3A), shared with BikePoses' first entry.
@res tables/riderstarts.bin

@module bike ; The bike: the controls, the lean and the steering, wheelies, jumps, falls and getting up, the collisions, off the road
@about SPEED ($7C33-$7C34, 8.8 km/h) rises with THROTTLE (fast at low speed, then by a fixed $220, slower from 160 toward 200: $C800, never reached, 199 the top) and falls with BRAKES ($580 a pass); RaceLoop applies them. ScrollStep = SPEED * 14 moves the track. The lean (-3 to 3) follows LEFT and RIGHT one step a pass and returns toward 0 every second pass without them; the sideways speed is SteerTable[speed] * LeanTable[lean] / 8 (its high byte capped at 5, the low byte kept), added to the lateral position LateralX with the bend's push (BikeLateral). WHEELIE held lifts the front (pose 0) and counts passes (Wheelies); at a ramp the speed is kept in proportion to the wheelie (8 passes: all of it; none: 3/4) and the jump's height comes from the ramp's place and the speed. Hitting an object or a truck at 35 km/h or more is a fall (Crash): the bike flies (JumpSpeed from the speed), lies for FallTime, the turn ends (the turn loop: the other player's turn, or a restart of this one at the fall's x); slower, and side-on hits, are a bounce (BounceTime: 3 passes of random steering). Off the road the speed is capped by the distance (OffRoad); stage 3's sand also makes the skid noise and dust, and its gap (the water) slows hard.
@about Poses (Pose, $9175): 0 wheelie, 1 and 2 in the air, 3 riding (the lean picks the sprite), 7 thrown, 8 and 9 down; BikePoses has the sprite of each (entries 3-6 the leans, mirrored for the other side).
$9127 b BikePoses 9 ; The bike's sprites: rows, width (bit 7 aligned), address, then the wheel animation (count, width, offset, source; count bit 7: none)
> DrawBike reads BikePoses + 9 * n + 1 ... + 9 (the code's base $9127). Entries with no animation leave their last four bytes free, and the game keeps variables in them: ScrollStep, $9138, LateralX, TopClip, DrawnTop, FallTime, Wheelies, Pose, Lean, RoadTopRow, BikeX.
$9136 ScrollStep ; SPEED * 14: the track's distance a pass (8.8)
$9138 BendPtr ; ScrollBends' profile pointer
$913F LateralX ; the bike's sideways place, 8.8 ($9140 the whole part, $5C-$A4 on the road's scale)
$916C TopClip ; the highest line drawn this pass (CopyBuffer starts there)
$916D DrawnTop ; last pass's TopClip
$916E FallTime ; a fall's count (bit 7: the side), 0 riding
$916F Wheelies ; passes with WHEELIE held
$9175 Pose ; the bike's pose
$9176 Lean ; the lean, -3 to 3
$9177 RoadTopRow ; RoadLines[127] - $71 (RowTable's first road line - $70); $FF breaks DrawRoad (the stage 3 reset)
$9178 BikeX ; the bike's screen x ($80 the centre)

@module objects
$917E b RiderSprites 21 ; Seven distance bands: the row threshold, then five poses' descriptors (rows | bit 7: never mirrored, width | bit 7 aligned, address)
> src/res/tables/riders.csv; the sprites in src/res/sprites/.
@res tables/riders.bin

@module horizon ; The horizons: the stage's picture above the road, rotated sideways in the bends
@about Three pictures, 32 bytes a line: Dunes ($E2C0, 25 lines, stage 3), Mountains ($E5E0, 32 lines, stages 1 and 4), Clouds ($E9E0, 9 lines, stages 2 and 5) (StageTable). DrawHorizon copies the stage's picture into the back buffer above the road and clears the lines between it and the top of what was drawn (ClearLines pushes zeros with SP as the pointer). On a curve the picture itself is rotated: HorizonScrollStep adds the curve's speed to HorizonScroll and rotates the picture in place by the whole pixels gained, through RotateTable: by 4 pixels (RRD / RLD) and by 1 (RR / RL), right or left; whole bytes (RotateBytesRight / Left) would serve steps of 8 and more, which never come.
$9211 c RotateRight1 ; Rotate C lines of the picture at HL right by one pixel (32 bytes a line, the last bit wraps to the first)
> Recorded: c1, stages: in every bend.
> Witness: L1 42826 (c1, crash, m128, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$923B c RotateLeft1 ; Rotate C lines left by one pixel (HL at a line's last byte; the next line 64 bytes on)
> Witness: L1 25206 (c1, crash, fall5, stages, truck).
$9268 c RotateRight4 ; Rotate C lines right by four pixels (RRD along the line, the last nibble wraps)
> Witness: L1 3033 (stages).
$9294 c RotateLeft4 ; Rotate C lines left by four pixels (RLD)
> Witness: L1 2100 (stages).
$92C0 c RotateBytesRight ; Rotate C lines right by whole bytes
> Known only from the code: a step of 8 pixels in one pass never happened (the bend's push stays below it).
$92ED c RotateBytesLeft ; Rotate C lines left by whole bytes
> Known only from the code.

$9316 b MaskTable ; Eight left-edge masks ($00, $80, $C0 ... $FE), read by no instruction
> Known only from the code: no operand points here and L1 has no read after the load; DotMasks ($9CAF) is the table the game uses.

@module road ; The road: its rows from the bends and crests, the drawing of the edges, the ground's dots, the bend table
@about A segment of StageList gives a bend (0-3, with a side) and a crest (0-3). Each pass a row builder (StageParams' second word, called at $A68B) makes RoadLines, the screen line of each of the 128 road rows: RoadRowsCrest (stages 1, 2, 4, 5) moves each row's screen line by the crest tables ($7A00) over the perspective table ($7800) as the segment goes by; RoadRowsJump (stage 3) lifts every row by the bike's height in the air; RoadRowsFlat is the straight. RoadRowsMake turns RoadLines into RowList (the rows to draw, top down) and RoadTopRow. BendRows (ScrollBends, BlendBend) shift each row sideways with the bend profiles ($7D80, $7E00, $7E80).
@about DrawRoad (StageParams' first word, called at $A68E) draws each row of RowList into the back buffer: the left and the right edge as 3-byte patterns (EdgePatterns: $7880 or $7A80 by the row's depth, 8 shifts each) at x = the row's centre (BendRows, the lateral position) minus and plus its half width (the RowScale tables at $7F00/$7F80), the road between left blank. It walks RowList with SP = RowTable + 2 * line, and Multiply's last byte is patched to a JP (MultiplyEnd) so that the multiply in each row returns without a RET, which would pop a line address. Stage 5 has no edges (RoadNone): its ground is GroundTexture's dots.
@about The bug of the stage 3 reset is here: RoadTopRow = RoadLines' last entry - $71, and with every row at line $70 (a jump so high that RoadRowsJump scales every row to 0) it is $FF; DrawRoad then sets SP = $FEE0 + 2 * $FF = $00DE, inside the ROM (NOTES, leftovers).
$931E c DrawRoad ; Draw the rows of RowList: both edges of each row into the back buffer, SP walking RowTable
> GapRowsJump first (GapRows: stage 3's gap rows become $FE in RowList); Multiply's RET patched to JP DrawRoadRow; SP = $FEE0 + 2 * RoadTopRow (RowTable from line $70 + RoadTopRow). Per entry: $FF ends, $FE skips a line (the gap), else the row's depth picks the edge pattern set and the width (RowScale), Multiply gives the hill's and the curve's shift, the left edge's 3 bytes are written (LDI, or LDDR clipped at the left), then the right edge's. Runs of equal entries (a row stretched over several lines) go to DrawRoadRun. At the end MultiplyEnd is RET again and SP restored.
> Recorded: c1: every pass; the heaviest passes' largest cost.
> Witness: L1 4740 (c1, crash, ctl, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
@isub $9321 LD IX,RowList
$9330 ; SP = RowTable + 2 * ($70 + RoadTopRow)
$9347 DrawRoadLoop ; the next RowList entry
$93AE ; JP Multiply: it comes back at DrawRoadRow by MultiplyEnd's JP
$93B1 DrawRoadRow ; the row's centre and left edge
$93F9 DrawRoadRight ; the right edge
$942F DrawRoadNext ; next line
$9439 DrawRoadEnd ; MultiplyEnd = RET; SP back
$9443 DrawRoadOff ; the row is off the road's table: skip its line
$9446 DrawRoadRun ; a run of lines of one row: the edges interpolated line by line (slopes by Divide)
$9562 b RoadRunOp ; One opcode the run writes per line: $BF (CP A) or $23 (INC HL), executed after POP DE
> DrawRoadRun writes it at $94B2 and $95F1. L1 has the byte executed in every recording; the kit keeps it as a byte because its instruction changes.
$9563 c DrawRoadRunLine ; One line of a run: the left edge, the right edge at the interpolated x, then the slopes added
> Witness: L1 142770 (c1, crash, ctl, m128, names, stages, tech-brake-bend, tech-cheat, tech-land-wheelie, tech-ramp-wheelie, truck, twoplayer).
$9601 c DrawRoadRunOff ; A run whose row is off the table: SP past its lines, MultiplyEnd = JP again, on to DrawRoadNext
> Known only from the code.

